Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 12:13:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Report diagnostics against the original source, not the
 preprocessed text

Groundwork for the AmigaIDE integration: an IDE needs to turn a compiler
error into a click that lands on the right line of the file the user is
editing.  Two things stood in the way.

ace derived a filename's extension from the FIRST dot, so "my.prog.b"
became "my.prog.b.b" and a directory containing a dot broke the .s name
derivation too.  find_extension() now takes the last dot of the last path
component.  This is a real bug fix on the Amiga as well, and it frees any
Makefile generator from having to invent dot-free intermediate names.

yap removes comments, folds blank lines and splices includes, so its
output stops tracking its input 1:1 and every ace diagnostic named a line
of the temp file - which the user never sees and cannot open.  yap -g now
emits "#line N "FILE"" whenever the position drifts, ace consumes those
markers in nextch(), and _error()/_warn() report the recorded position.
bas passes -g unconditionally, so the command line benefits too.

The message text is extended additively: without markers it stays
byte-identical to before, so CubicIDE and anything else parsing bas -E
output keeps working.  With markers it reads

  ** Undefined function or array not dimensioned in line 3 of "incd/helper.b".

including for errors inside an included file, which was previously
impossible to attribute at all.

Verified: 12/12 yap tests (new test_linemarkers covers -g in both the
host and the ARexx runner), all 223 non-error cases still build and all
28 errors/ cases are still rejected, amiga-check still builds, and
compare-host-vs-amiga.sh still reports the same 192 identical .s files -
the markers change diagnostics only, never the emitted assembly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
